#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157691 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffe4292f-7653-5486-975e-c206acae734e" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of Big-O"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "971de907-aa22-5e54-86e1-730856500bc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the sum property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155316} {:answer/id #uuid "adeb3a38-8e02-56f1-833f-d049b423ba2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155317} {:answer/id #uuid "b4d323a2-e647-521b-99a9-c99cacd126af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the absorption property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155318} {:answer/id #uuid "b7ad2711-3588-50f5-80fa-45a5f56319fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the transitive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155319} {:answer/id #uuid "38fa238c-1f65-57b3-9eaa-dde23ccc163d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the constant multiple property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155320}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38fa238c-1f65-57b3-9eaa-dde23ccc163d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the constant multiple property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155320} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c3ccbab-e46c-5e80-aa80-cb21b6d4a36f" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155315} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d4ebb7c-62d1-58fa-a8c9-d2e9996cdea3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the sum property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155325} {:answer/id #uuid "ded68a95-0a78-59ea-bb21-e9cfc20deb9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155326} {:answer/id #uuid "0dceb9f9-6fe6-5e84-be8b-c8b97a20a7e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the absorption property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155327} {:answer/id #uuid "3e78c090-60be-5986-9ec9-f7914a293d12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the transitive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155328} {:answer/id #uuid "46f04aef-bce5-5182-bec8-b656dc8495c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the constant multiple property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155329}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0dceb9f9-6fe6-5e84-be8b-c8b97a20a7e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the absorption property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155327} :answer-field/id #uuid "f60f26d3-d734-5423-9bae-c9cd95e8d1ec" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155324} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c83ae94d-8aa1-56d6-bb5d-258be8eeaed1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the constant multiple property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155331} {:answer/id #uuid "e43b51bb-1cd8-55ae-a203-edf7630b8523" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the absorption property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155332} {:answer/id #uuid "16afd9e4-afc2-5f01-a536-d2255c8fbfd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155333} {:answer/id #uuid "6b26beea-fddf-5b32-84c2-afd8ec818462" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the sum property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155334} {:answer/id #uuid "76d81dc6-7bc3-51f1-872e-d15ba95a6c21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the transitive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155335}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16afd9e4-afc2-5f01-a536-d2255c8fbfd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155333} :answer-field/id #uuid "27d6a495-8e1a-5be4-a327-1398919f82b4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155330} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "349f4267-da67-5757-bbdc-0ba417f2bfaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317447} {:answer/id #uuid "f0910e26-a1cc-544c-933d-8bffcf750c16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0910e26-a1cc-544c-933d-8bffcf750c16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317448} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4f49e08-37d3-5c47-9ff1-2d8d6d98f56b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317446} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6e20e85-df84-572f-bdc7-d1c656459385" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317450} {:answer/id #uuid "c880982f-b317-5ac7-be74-1e6817e910d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317451} {:answer/id #uuid "0e73028b-f838-5a5c-a6ee-0279c4c0fb7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317452}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6e20e85-df84-572f-bdc7-d1c656459385" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317450} :answer-field/id #uuid "7204e117-ab7d-593f-a243-21b152e0792d" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317449}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "189a55f5-72ee-590d-b213-1f6bb96fbc77" :question/math-academy-id "q-334685" :question/problem "Consider the following statement. \n\nIf $f(x)=3{e}^{2x}+2x,$ then $f=O({e}^{2x})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nA proof of the statement is given below\n\n $\\text{L1}:\\,f=O(3{e}^{2x}+2x)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2x}{3{e}^{2x}})=0$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,2x=O(3{e}^{2x})$ \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,f=O(3{e}^{2x})$  \n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,f=O({e}^{2x})$ \n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L1}$ follows from {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from lines \n{{field-2}} and \n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from line {{field-4}}  and \n{{field-5}}." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x),g(x),h(x)$ and $k(x)$ are functions and $c$ is a nonzero constant. Then, we have the following properties of Big-O notation. \n\n- The sum and product properties. If\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(k)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n \nthen\n\n$$\nf+g=O(h+k)\\,\\text{and}\\,fg=O(hk)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The constant multiple property. If \n\n$$\nf=O(cg)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n \nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The reflexive property. \n\n$$\nf=O(f)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n- The transitive property. If \n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The absorption property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(g+h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n \nthen \n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L1},$ which states that\n\n$$\nf=O({{3{e}^{2x}+2x}_{⏟}}_{f})\n$$\n\nand this is true by $\\text{the reflexive property}.$\n\n- Next, we consider line $\\text{L4}.$ From line $\\text{L1},$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O(3{e}^{2x}+2x)\n$$\n\nand from line $\\text{L3},$ we have\n\n$$\n2x=O(3{e}^{2x}).\n$$\n\nThus, by lines $\\text{L1 and L3}$ and the $\\text{absorption property},$ we have \n\n$$\nf=O(3{e}^{2x}+2x)=O(3{e}^{2x}).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider line $\\text{L5}.$ From line $\\text{L4},$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O(3{e}^{2x})\n$$\n\nand by the $\\text{constant multiple property}$ with $c=3,$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O({e}^{2x}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317453 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b841cdab-5473-5eeb-a02a-1d1acbae2984" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Limit Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf7741d2-e17a-5c4d-8397-349a628d5d5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317455} {:answer/id #uuid "a1df42cf-97e2-5dd5-abd8-c7fed6c55755" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317456} {:answer/id #uuid "bb13d96c-8d18-5f0d-a347-114df2e6939e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317457} {:answer/id #uuid "c4e00d0b-5434-5641-b8a9-0a0134d8e6dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317458} {:answer/id #uuid "09119b98-d188-5b07-8b43-8708edf41cfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317459}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4e00d0b-5434-5641-b8a9-0a0134d8e6dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317458} :answer-field/id #uuid "2df209c0-e566-5903-a5c3-43cf640a7193" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "495263d0-1877-5f8f-8096-f99ff3c5910b" :question/math-academy-id "q-334862" :question/problem "Suppose $f(x)=4{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$\n- $f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0$\n- $f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=4{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x).\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(4{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let $g(x)=x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(4x+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)}{x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNear $x=0,$ we have $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)≈x,$ so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)}{x}≈1.\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(4x+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)}{x})=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{2}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(4+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)}{{x}^{2}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)≈x$ near $x=0,$ the quotient \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)}{{x}^{2}}≈\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nbecomes arbitrarily large in magnitude as $x\\to 0.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(4+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)}{{x}^{2}})=\\infty .\n$$\n\nThus, $f\\ne O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317460 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b841cdab-5473-5eeb-a02a-1d1acbae2984" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Limit Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53b62250-3acf-5d32-95e5-f6d5b7949b88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317462} {:answer/id #uuid "329ece97-a84d-59cc-aaeb-648dfb55b68e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317463} {:answer/id #uuid "d89321e7-cd05-5d26-a5d8-72fb2f924774" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317464} {:answer/id #uuid "b2aa6cc5-98b0-5c89-8d13-a8dff925cdda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317465} {:answer/id #uuid "aaf1e78b-c38d-5f1e-bfe1-269b6c1212bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317466}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aaf1e78b-c38d-5f1e-bfe1-269b6c1212bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317466} :answer-field/id #uuid "30a1fc37-f60a-5996-b781-bee4bd76887c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317461}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "935514fa-c825-5eb7-b399-b6078ffdcb71" :question/math-academy-id "q-334870" :question/problem "Suppose $f(x)=-5{x}^{6}+9{x}^{4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- $f=O({x}^{8})$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- $f=O({x}^{5})$ as $x\\to \\infty $" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the limit\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the polynomial\n\n$$\nf(x)=-5{x}^{6}+9{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, let us analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-5{x}^{6}+9{x}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let $g(x)={x}^{8}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-5{x}^{6}+9{x}^{4}}{{x}^{8}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{9}{{x}^{4}}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({x}^{8})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement III is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{5}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-5{x}^{6}+9{x}^{4}}{{x}^{5}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-5x+\\frac{9}{{x}^{4}}) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O({x}^{5})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317467 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b841cdab-5473-5eeb-a02a-1d1acbae2984" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Limit Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd894485-76ee-56a9-bd6f-fcbb82ba3707" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317469} {:answer/id #uuid "1fb04016-4c6f-571e-9d79-464723729f6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317470} {:answer/id #uuid "8bee31b7-8fe3-5a32-aa35-c746efe5bd4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317471} {:answer/id #uuid "93378f84-11ca-5ccd-a307-6dafe525f28a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317472} {:answer/id #uuid "d3b6e740-a129-5dcc-9b06-cb1243436560" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fb04016-4c6f-571e-9d79-464723729f6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317470} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac4d6ac6-ff38-5420-a7bb-819006909473" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317468}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "258f80bc-2f49-5faa-a101-ac0575f4220f" :question/math-academy-id "q-335019" :question/problem "Suppose $f(x)=3{x}^{2}-x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- $f=O(x{e}^{x})$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- $f=O({x}^{3})$ as $x\\to \\infty $" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the limit\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the polynomial\n\n$$\nf(x)=3{x}^{2}-x.\n$$\n\nNow, let us analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(3{x}^{2}-x) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }x(3x-1) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let $g(x)=x{e}^{x}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{2}-x}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x(3x-1)}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3x-1}{{e}^{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince now direct evaluation leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ we can evaluate the limit using L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3x-1}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(3x-1)^{′}}{({e}^{x})^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O(x{e}^{x})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let $g(x)={x}^{3}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{2}-x}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{3}{x}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({x}^{8})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317474 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffe4292f-7653-5486-975e-c206acae734e" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of Big-O"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7bb0c48-01cf-5cc2-9592-07663a7244b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the absorption property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317476} {:answer/id #uuid "f902526a-f98d-5525-b3ac-f09eeaf021b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the constant multiple property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317477} {:answer/id #uuid "d7ad6837-ae05-5343-8a9e-85f62dbe5dc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the sum property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317478} {:answer/id #uuid "2da3dc02-ac99-5913-84d6-58af90220ab8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the transitive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317479} {:answer/id #uuid "56b09e62-830d-536a-9756-ee6fa5b96933" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317480}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56b09e62-830d-536a-9756-ee6fa5b96933" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317480} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdea836c-8df6-5cfd-9796-7d64a14eba5b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317475} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21d1c791-3184-5aec-a9a0-8db69f06352f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317482} {:answer/id #uuid "57b16a35-6a58-5798-a827-4e271be90d77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317483}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57b16a35-6a58-5798-a827-4e271be90d77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317483} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb7cf48f-e4ad-5034-acc2-1d46ea2821b2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317481} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f655a6e8-47f6-5816-9e1c-f0cb9519d635" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the absorption property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317485} {:answer/id #uuid "3c2618a9-6e9b-5407-9e04-b06261f85d6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the constant multiple property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317486} {:answer/id #uuid "5715d567-39e9-53d3-ae52-a3996d78e696" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the sum property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317487} {:answer/id #uuid "ce004672-78f6-5644-acdf-98c3dc22ba68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the transitive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317488} {:answer/id #uuid "df6e46da-c0d0-5761-a7fa-f699bc3aadd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f655a6e8-47f6-5816-9e1c-f0cb9519d635" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the absorption property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317485} :answer-field/id #uuid "db2e326f-afd3-5d83-87c5-1b586274eb42" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317484} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f02aa4eb-8ce7-5ed1-865f-ae5b92e1f5dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317491} {:answer/id #uuid "fd73f777-a152-5053-a700-09416c17c9e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317492}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd73f777-a152-5053-a700-09416c17c9e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317492} :answer-field/id #uuid "60068933-6646-54f0-9df5-2e346255320d" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317490} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b6b16a4-d497-5b1a-b500-8e3ea9c495a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the absorption property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317494} {:answer/id #uuid "9fb0d32b-a8ec-5fba-b383-a42c027db5cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the constant multiple property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317495} {:answer/id #uuid "9c024d47-b408-5e92-bcce-d5215b58421d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the sum property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317496} {:answer/id #uuid "2375bab7-dfdb-5100-a73f-fe1f5ab2d9eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the transitive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317497} {:answer/id #uuid "99cf03b8-8f7b-50da-9f3a-6ed00ac0e990" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317498}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2375bab7-dfdb-5100-a73f-fe1f5ab2d9eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the transitive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317497} :answer-field/id #uuid "99380087-c3bb-54f0-8397-985af8fdab73" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317493}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f2dd9dd4-da44-5101-8af9-d9e06c698ad1" :question/math-academy-id "q-335372" :question/problem "Consider the following statement. \n\nIf $f(x)={x}^{2}+3{x}^{3},$ then $f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nA proof of the statement is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,f=O({x}^{2}+3{x}^{3})$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,3{x}^{3}=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0$ \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0$ \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,{x}^{2}=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0$ \n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,f=O(x)$  as $x\\to 0$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L1}$ follows from {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from lines \n{{field-2}} and \n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines {{field-4}}  and \n{{field-5}}." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x),g(x),h(x)$ and $k(x)$ are functions and $c$ is a nonzero constant. Then, we have the following properties of Big-O notation. \n\n- The sum and product properties. If\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(k)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf+g=O(h+k)\\,\\text{and}\\,fg=O(hk)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The constant multiple property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(cg)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The reflexive property.\n\n$$\nf=O(f)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The transitive property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The absorption property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(g+h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let us justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L1},$ which states that\n\n$$\nf=O({{{x}^{2}+3{x}^{3}}_{⏟}}_{f}).\n$$\n\nThis is true by $\\text{the reflexive property}.$\n\n- Next, we consider line $\\text{L3}.$ From line $\\text{L1}$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O({x}^{2}+3{x}^{3}),\n$$\n\nand from line $\\text{L2}$ we have\n\n$$\n3{x}^{3}=O({x}^{2})\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThus, by lines $\\text{L1 and L2}$ and the $\\text{absorption property},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nf=O({x}^{2})\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider line $\\text{L5}.$ From line  $\\text{L3},$   we have\n\n$$\nf=O({x}^{2})\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0,\n$$\n\nand from line  $\\text{L4},$   we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=O(x)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThus, by lines $\\text{L3 and L4}$ and by $\\text{the transitive property},$  we conclude that\n\n$$\nf=O(x)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$"}]}
